1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:13,359
Austral winter off Lady Elliot Island, southern Great Barrier Reef. You rest on coral sand ten meters down.

2
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:32,853
With no open-circuit scuba bubbles to startle wildlife, the reef soundscape emerges: snapping shrimp and grazing parrotfish.

3
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,056
Straight ahead on the bommie, bluestreak cleaner wrasse hold station in the weak tidal current.

4
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,135
Look up. Slowly.

5
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,793
A reef manta ray, Mobula alfredi, glides in from astern and passes directly overhead.

6
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,599
Between June and August, hundreds of mantas aggregate around Lady Elliot's nutrient-rich Capricorn Bunker waters.

7
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,945
Each pectoral stroke is effortless hydrodynamic lift across a wingspan exceeding three and a half meters.

8
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,318
Straight ahead, the manta stalls into a near-stationary hover above the bommie, unrolling its cephalic lobes.

9
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:06,369
This is classic cleaning mutualism: Labroides dimidiatus remove parasitic copepods and necrotic tissue from skin and gill arches.

10
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:28,968
Notice the black markings on its white ventrum. Project Manta researchers use those lifelong belly spots to identify individuals across decades.

11
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,378
Pan slowly around the blue water column. Two more mantas queue in wide holding circles around the station.

12
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,599
Farther back in mid-water, a resident green turtle, Chelonia mydas, cruises toward the coral cay lagoon.

13
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,463
Site-faithful mantas return repeatedly to the same coral outcrops year after year before dispersing to feed on zooplankton.

14
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,126
Sunlight refracts down from the surface swells, closing another unhurried session on the bommie.

15
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:23,461
